# Feature-card taxonomy and sync rules

## Manual edits

* A manual tag lock protects existing manual tags only; it must not block adding a new matching system-generated tag.
* A manual category lock prevents automatic movement to another category, but it must not block adding a new matching system-generated tag.
* Never interpret a manual override as a reason to skip the entire feature card.
* Treat manually edited tags, categories, names, and card placement as explicit user decisions.
* Never automatically remove, replace, normalize, or reassign a manually locked tag or category.
* Store manual overrides separately from automatically generated values.
* Record the editor, timestamp, previous value, new value, and reason for every manual override.
* If a card has a manual category override, automatic scans must not move it.
* If a card has manually edited tags, preserve those tags during every automatic sync.

## Category creation

* Tesla categories may classify only Tesla feature cards.
* Rivian categories may classify only Rivian feature cards.
* When a new category is added, scan all feature cards for the matching brand.
* Use explicit category rules, aliases, keywords, and feature metadata for matching.
* Do not classify from vague semantic similarity alone.
* Add new matching system-generated tags without removing manual tags.
* Preserve manually locked categories.
* Allow multiple categories unless the product explicitly requires one category only.
* Do not run a full reclassification after a normal card edit.
* Category scans must be idempotent and must not create duplicate tags or cards.

## Category renaming

* Treat a category rename as an explicit migration.
* Update the canonical category name and system-managed references.
* Replace old tags only when they were system-generated.
* If an old tag was manually edited or locked, preserve it and add the new canonical tag.
* Before applying a rename, generate a dry-run summary of affected cards.
* Record the migration version, timestamp, operator, and rollback information.
* Do not rename similarly named categories unless explicitly requested.

## Tesla and Rivian source monitoring

* Cursor rules alone cannot monitor websites in the background.
* Website monitoring must run through a scheduled job, worker, cron task, or CI workflow.
* Monitor only the configured Tesla and Rivian release-note websites.
* Respect robots.txt, terms of use, authentication requirements, and rate limits.
* Store the source URL, retrieval time, publication date, content hash, and parser version.
* Detect revisions using stable source identifiers and content hashes.
* Never create duplicate feature cards when a source page changes.
* Update only fields owned by the source-sync process.
* Never overwrite manually edited fields.
* Send ambiguous, malformed, inaccessible, or materially changed content to a review queue.
* Do not silently delete cards when a source page disappears.
* Validate all imported data before publishing it.
* Use staged or transactional updates with rollback support.
* Report added, changed, skipped, rejected, and review-required records.
* Log sync status, failures, retries, and the last successful sync.

## Design protection

* Preserve the current visual design, layout, navigation, spacing, typography, colors, and responsive behavior.
* Do not change UI or CSS unless the user explicitly requests a design change.
* Data synchronization must not redesign the website.
* If a data change requires a UI change, make the smallest possible change and explain its impact.
* Do not rewrite unrelated components or perform broad refactors.

## Data model and provenance

* Use stable IDs for feature cards and categories.
* Do not use display names as primary keys.
* Store automatic and manual values separately.
* Prefer per-tag and per-category locks instead of one global override when possible.
* Store source references and source update timestamps.
* Maintain category aliases and rename mappings in version-controlled configuration.
* Validate that each category belongs to the correct brand.
* Define and consistently apply rules for case, whitespace, spelling, aliases, and localization.
* Include a schema version for stored feature-card data.

## Safety and testing

* Add tests for manual overrides and locked categories.
* Add tests for Tesla and Rivian category creation.
* Add tests for multi-category matches and duplicate prevention.
* Add tests for category rename migrations.
* Add tests proving automatic sync cannot move manually categorized cards.
* Use dry-run mode for bulk scans and migrations.
* Require approval before destructive changes, bulk moves, manual-value replacement, or deletion.
* Maintain an audit log for manual and automated changes.
* Support rollback for migrations and source imports.
* Use feature flags for new classifiers, parsers, and taxonomy migrations.
* Fail safely when matching confidence, validation, or source integrity checks fail.
* Never expose API keys, cookies, passwords, or private credentials in logs or client-side code.
* Sanitize imported HTML and text before displaying it.
* Use timeouts, retry backoff, rate limiting, and circuit breakers for source monitoring.
* Make sync jobs resumable so one bad page cannot block all updates.

## Coding-agent workflow

Before changing the implementation:

1. Inspect the existing data model, category logic, sync jobs, and tests.
2. Identify system-managed fields and manually overridable fields.
3. Plan changes that affect taxonomy, migrations, or source synchronization.
4. Update or add tests with the implementation.
5. Run targeted tests, type checks, linting, and a production build when applicable.
6. Report changed files, migration effects, test results, and records requiring review.
7. Do not redesign the interface or modify unrelated files.
